freesurferformats Changes
=========================


Version 1.1.0 -- Track file readers, subsetting, streaming and writing
---------------------------------------------------------------------
- FIX: `read.dti.tck()` and `read.dti.tsf()` now support gzip-compressed files (`.tck.gz`, `.tsf.gz`). They used to return garbage (plausible-looking tracks with coordinates around +/-3.4e38) without any error, because the header was decompressed transparently but the payload was read from a raw connection, so compressed bytes were interpreted as coordinates. QSIRecon and MRtrix pipelines write `.tck.gz`, and the whole-brain tractogram of a single subject can easily exceed 8 GB. Compression is now detected from the gzip magic bytes rather than the file name, so renamed files work too.
- FIX: `read.dti.tck()` and `read.dti.tsf()` no longer derive the payload size from the file size, which is meaningless for compressed files. Reading now validates the header (first line, `END`, `datatype`, `file` entry, data offset) and stops at the end-of-file marker instead.
- FIX: Remove the 100 KB minimum file size heuristic in `read.dti.tck()`, which rejected valid small TCK/TSF files. A valid 130 byte file with 2 streamlines is now read.
- FIX: The TCK/TSF header is no longer limited to 50 lines. Real headers from e.g. QSIRecon contain a `command_history` entry of arbitrary length, and any header longer than 50 lines used to fail with 'invalid format'. Header values may now contain colons as well.
- FIX: The TCK/TSF `count` header entry is optional, as documented by MRtrix (only `file` and `datatype` are required). Files without it caused an 'argument is of length zero' error, and files with a count larger than 2^31-1 caused 'missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed'.
- FIX: `read.dti.trk()` now reads all tracks until the end of the file when the `n_count` header field is 0, which per the TRK spec means that the track count was not stored. It used to silently return zero tracks for such files, i.e., it lost all data without a warning. A stored count that is larger than the data present is now reported as a truncated file.
- FIX: The track data in `read.dti.trk()` was read point by point (3 or more separate `readBin()` calls per point). Each track record is now read with a single `readBin()` call and split afterwards, which makes reading about 10x faster.
- FIX: `get.dti.trk.endianness()` crashed with 'argument is of length zero' for files shorter than 1000 bytes instead of reporting that the file is not in TRK format.
- ENH: `read.dti.trk()` and `read.dti.tck()` accept a new `max_tracks` parameter to read only the first N tracks. This makes it possible to inspect a very large tractogram without holding all of it in memory.
- ENH: The tracks returned by `read.dti.trk()` and `read.dti.tck()` are now stored in a compact container of class `fs.tracts` instead of a plain list with one matrix per track. All coordinates are kept in a single matrix plus an integer vector with the number of points per track, which is the same layout that `nibabel` uses (its `ArraySequence` class) and that the TRX file format stores on disk. This removes the per-track overhead of roughly 100 bytes plus one allocation, which dominates both runtime and memory for the millions of tracks found in whole-brain tractograms. **This is an API change**: `tracks[[i]]`, `length(tracks)` and `lapply()` keep working as before, but `tracks` is no longer a plain `list`, so code that inspects the object itself (e.g. `names()` or `str()`) needs adjusting. Use `as.list()` to get the old representation, or the new accessors `fs.tracts.coords()` and `fs.tracts.lengths()` and the predicate `is.fs.tracts()`. Subsetting with `[` returns an `fs.tracts` object.
- FIX: `read.fs.volume.nii()` now returns the voxel data in the raw NIFTI file storage order by default. Its `...` parameters used to be passed on to `oro.nifti::readNIfTI()`, whose default is `reorient = TRUE`. That reorientation permutes and/or flips the voxel data, but does not update the `sform` header fields, so the returned `data` array did not match the geometry derived from these fields in the returned header (and thus not `mghheader.vox2ras()`). This silently yielded wrong results for all NIFTI files whose `sform` is not aligned with the RAS axes, e.g. `inst/extdata/vol27int.nii.gz` or scanner-space data in LAS orientation, and it made volumes with an oblique (rotated) `sform` unreadable, since `oro.nifti` aborted with the error "Transformation is not simple, cannot reorient!".
- NEW: `read.fs.volume.nii()` has an explicit `reorient` parameter now (default `FALSE`) instead of relying on `...`. Reorienting on read, and passing a `nifti` instance that was read with `reorient = TRUE`, are discouraged and now raise a warning.
- NEW: `read.fs.volume.nii()` returns a `vox2ras_matrix` entry in the header if the NIFTI file contains `sform` or `qform` geometry information, just like `read.fs.mgh()` does.

- NEW: `read.dti.tck.header()`, `read.dti.tsf.header()` and `read.dti.trk.header()` read only the file header and never touch the track data. Reading the header of a 9 GB tractogram takes about 2 milliseconds, so a file can be inspected (or rejected early) before any decision about reading tracks is made. The TCK/TSF header is returned as a list, and the TRK header includes the derived `vox2ras` and `vox2ras_corrected` matrices that `read.dti.trk()` computes with `shift_origin = TRUE`.
- NEW: `read.dti.tck()`, `read.dti.tsf()` and `read.dti.trk()` accept a new `skip_tracks` and `bbox` parameter. The bounding box is given as `c(xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax, zmin, zmax)` and keeps every track that has at least one point inside it, which is what is needed to extract a region of interest (e.g. one hemisphere, or a box around a lesion) from a whole-brain tractogram. Both filters are applied while reading, so tracks that are skipped are never held in memory. The `bbox` is interpreted in the coordinate system of the coordinates that the function returns, i.e. in RAS space when `coords = 'ras'` is used for TRK files. Note that the number of tracks inside a box cannot be known before reading, so for very large files `bbox` should be combined with a `max_tracks` value that is known to be safe.
- NEW: `dti.track.count()`, `dti.track.bbox()` and `dti.track.iterator()`. The first two scan a track file (TCK, TSF or TRK) once, in chunks, and report the number of tracks and the exact bounding box of all streamline coordinates, without ever holding a track in memory. This makes it possible to check the `count` entry of the header of a huge file, which is often missing or wrong. The iterator hands out one track at a time via `itr$next.track()` (and `NULL` at the end), with a constant memory footprint: iterating over 10 million tracks uses the same few dozen MB as iterating over the first 100, since only one chunk is buffered. It supports `skip_tracks` and `bbox` as well, and the connection is closed automatically when the iterator is garbage collected, or explicitly via `itr$close()`. For TRK files the count scan uses the fixed record size to skip over the tracks, so it is fast even for large files.
- NEW: `write.dti.tck()` and `write.dti.trk()` write `fs.tracts` instances back to TCK and TRK files. This complements the readers, so a subset of a huge tractogram can now be cropped out and saved: `write.dti.tck(read.dti.tck(f, max_tracks = 2000)$tracks, 'crop.tck')`. Coordinates, per-track lengths, and the file header are preserved exactly, and gzip compression can be requested per file name or explicitly. For TRK files the per-point scalars, the per-track properties and the big endian variant are supported as well. `write.dti.trk()` needs the geometry for the header: pass the header of a file that was read, or the result of `dti.track.bbox()`, or write with `coords_space = 'native'`.
- NEW: The `fs.tracts` constructor is now exported, along with `as.fs.tracts()` which converts the legacy list-of-matrices representation (as returned by `read.dti.trk()` in version 1.0.x) into an `fs.tracts` object. Together with the writers above this allows constructing and saving tractograms from scratch.
- ENH: `read.dti.trk()` has a new `coords` parameter and can now return the track coordinates in RAS space (`coords = 'ras'`, the space the tractography was performed in) instead of the raw coordinates stored in the file. The transformation is the one used by `nibabel` (including the `voxel_order` reconciliation), not a naive application of the `vox2ras` matrix from the header: for the XTRACT example file from the issue tracker the two differ by half a voxel. The default (`coords = NULL`) keeps returning the stored coordinates, but now warns if the `vox2ras` matrix in the header is not the identity, since the coordinates are then not in RAS space. Pass `coords = 'native'` to get the old behavior without the warning.
- FIX: A missing `Inf` end-of-file marker in a TCK/TSF file is no longer an error. MRtrix itself accepts the end of the file as the end of the data (the `in.eof()` check in its reader), and a missing terminator is now only reported if the `count` entry of the header shows that tracks are missing. Writing a TCK file with `count: 0` (an empty tractogram) used to make the reader abort with 'does not contain any streamlines'; such files are now read as an empty result.
- FIX: A `vox_to_ras` matrix that is all zeros in a TRK file means that the transformation was not recorded. It is now treated as the identity matrix (with a warning), as `nibabel` does, instead of being applied as a degenerate matrix that collapsed all coordinates onto the origin.
- FIX: `dti.track.count()`, `dti.track.bbox()` and `dti.track.iterator()` refused to open an uncompressed tract file whose payload exceeds the memory limit, e.g. a 9 GB tractogram, with 'Requested allocation of 19.45 GB ... exceeds the safety limit'. They stream through the file and never hold it in memory, so that check must not apply to them, and it is now only used for whole-file reads. The same applied to `read.dti.tck(..., max_tracks = N)`, which asks for a subset and could nevertheless be rejected because of the size of the whole file, so a huge file could not be read at all, not even the first track. The up-front check also opened the file before validating it, which left a file connection behind when it aborted.
- FIX: A TRK file that contains a track without any point (which the format allows) was read incorrectly: the reader did not consume the properties of such a track, lost the sync with the file and then reported garbage, and it did not count the track, so `read.dti.trk()` could report a perfectly valid file as truncated. Empty tracks are now returned (as `nibabel` does), their properties are read, and they are counted by `read.dti.trk()`, `dti.track.count()`, `dti.track.bbox()` and `dti.track.iterator()`. Note that a `bbox` filter drops them, since none of their (zero) points can be inside a box. This also means that a TRK file written from an `fs.tracts` instance that contains empty tracks round-trips exactly, which `write.dti.trk()` was already writing.
- FIX: `write.dti.tck()` now warns when the tracks to write contain tracks without points. The TCK format cannot represent them unambiguously: they are written as a bare delimiter, which every reader drops again (this package and `nibabel` behave the same), so the file would silently read back with fewer tracks than it was written from. Use `write.dti.trk()` if empty tracks have to be preserved.
- FIX: The memory limit message reported small limits and sizes in GB, so that e.g. a 48 KB payload was reported as 'Requested allocation of 0.00 GB ... exceeds the safety limit of 0.00 GB'. Sizes below 1 GB are now reported in bytes, KB or MB.
- ENH: The TCK/TSF readers and the streaming helpers take an advanced `chunk_values` parameter, the number of payload values that are read per chunk. It does not change the result, only the peak memory usage and the I/O granularity, so it is rarely needed; the default of 4e6 values corresponds to about 32 MB. Lower it on a machine with very little free memory.
- ENH: The test suite now covers the code paths that the multi-GB tractograms of real pipelines depend on without shipping any large file: the same small file is read with a dozen different chunk sizes, the memory limit is lowered so that the check triggers on a small file, a sparse file that reports 4 GB while occupying a few KB of disk is used to test the size-dependent branch, and the header of a real 8.9 GB QSIRecon/MRtrix3 tractogram is embedded to test parsing a realistic header. See the new `tests/testthat/test-dti-large-file-handling.R`.


Version 1.0.2 -- Improved CIFTI support and security
-----------------------------------------------------
- NEW: support reading CIFTI dlabel (surface parcellation) and dtseries (surface time series) files via the 'cifti' package: new functions `read.fs.parcellation.cifti()` and `read.fs.series.cifti()`. The shared surface-extraction logic of `read.fs.morph.cifti()` was refactored into internal helpers.
- ENH: keep additional CIFTI test data (dlabel, dtseries, ptseries) in `extra_test_data` (excluded from the package via `.Rbuildignore`, since it would exceed the 5 MB CRAN size limit), and add a dev script to fetch it.
- SEC: add configurable max memory allocation via R option or env var, and other sanity fixes
- NEW: support reading a brain atlas from a LUT and region info in a CSV file. also support writing to these files.
- FIX: Fix the RAS geometry written by `write.fs.mgh` when a `vox2ras` matrix with anisotropic voxels whose axes are not aligned with the coordinate axes is given. The direction cosines (`Mdc`) are now normalized per column, i.e., each voxel size is applied to its own column, instead of being misaligned, which previously corrupted the stored orientation. This is a rare special case.
- ENH: Add tests that cross-validate the FreeSurfer reference files `brain.mgz` (shipped in `inst/extdata`) and `brain.nii` (kept in `extra_test_data`, which is not part of the package due to the 5 MB size limit). The tests check that reading both files yields identical voxel data and geometry, and that a NIfTI header generated from the MGH volume matches the FreeSurfer NIfTI reference. They are skipped when `extra_test_data` is not available (CRAN, installed package) and run in git checkouts of the repository, including continuous integration.


Version 1.0.1 -- Nifti conversion support and fixes
---------------------------------------------------
- FIX: Fix missing null bytes (string termination markers) when writing string in fs annot files, issue #34.
- ENH: Add `shift_origin` parameter to `read.dti.trk()` to optionally compute the corrected vox2ras matrix (the TRK file stores an mm2ras matrix, not a true vox2ras). Fixes issue #35.
- ENH: Add support for converting MGH/MGZ volumes to NIFTI v1 format, issue #28. New functions: `nii1header.for.mgh()` to create a NIFTI v1 header from an MGH header, and `write.fs.volume()` to write `fs.volume` instances to MGH, MGZ, or NIFTI v1 files. Includes internal helpers `nifti.dtypebitpix.info.from.mgh.dtype()` and `m44_to_quaternion()`. New tests added.
- FIX: Fix potential crash in `read.dti.tsf()` and `read.dti.tck()` when a file header contains duplicate keys, causing a "factor level duplicated" error in `.read.dti.tcktsf`.
- ENH: The optional test data can now be downloaded from a GitHub release as an alternative source, with automatic fallback to the rcmd.org server if unavailable.
- FIX: Remove reference to non-existent `tracks.trk` file from the optional test data listing.
- SEC: Default to https when downloading optional data


Version 1.0.0 -- CRAN maintenance only
---------------------------------------
- Fix some very minor docstring formatting issues, as required by new checks on CRAN.
- Freesurferformats is now stable enough to move the version number to v1.0.0.
- Note: There are NO breaking changes from the last release. The jump in version number is only to indicate that this is now pretty feature complete and stable.


